    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nBlue-green: two identical environments, one active (blue) and one idle (green). Switch traffic after validation. Canary: incremental rollout (e.g., 5%, 20%, 100%) to reduce risk and test performance.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between blue-green deployment and canary deployment and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Blue-green: two identical environments, one active (blue) and one idle (green).\n2. Switch traffic after validation.\n3. Canary: incremental rollout (e.g., 5%, 20%, 100%) to reduce risk and test performance.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Blue-green: two identical environments, one active (blue) and one idle (green).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Blue-green: two identical environments, one active (blue) and one idle (green).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
