    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse a key-value store to map short codes to long URLs. Generate short codes using base62 encoding of a counter or hash of the URL. Implement a caching layer (Redis) for fast redirects. Use a CDN for static content.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how would you design a url shortener (like bit.ly), including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use a key-value store to map short codes to long URLs.\n2. Generate short codes using base62 encoding of a counter or hash of the URL.\n3. Implement a caching layer (Redis) for fast redirects.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Use a key-value store to map short codes to long URLs.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Use a key-value store to map short codes to long URLs.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
