    slug: "circuit-breaker-vs-bulkhead",
    shortDescription: "Explain the two resilience patterns.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCircuit breaker prevents repeated failures by short-circuiting calls. Bulkhead isolates resources (e.g., thread pools) so that a failure in one part does not affect others.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a circuit breaker and a bulkhead pattern and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Circuit breaker prevents repeated failures by short-circuiting calls.\n2. Bulkhead isolates resources (e.g., thread pools) so that a failure in one part does not affect others.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Circuit breaker prevents repeated failures by short-circuiting calls.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Circuit breaker prevents repeated failures by short-circuiting calls.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
