    question: "How do you test a distributed system for reliability?",
    slug: "test-distributed-system",
    shortDescription: "Discuss testing techniques.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUse chaos engineering to inject failures, performance testing (load, stress), integration testing with fakes/mocks, and end-to-end testing. Also, test failure scenarios like network partitions and node crashes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you test a distributed system for reliability, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Use chaos engineering to inject failures, performance testing (load, stress), integration testing with fakes/mocks, and end-to-end testing.\n2. Also, test failure scenarios like network partitions and node crashes.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain the procedure as a state change rather than a list of commands. Say what changes at each important step and how you would verify it.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Use chaos engineering to inject failures, performance testing (load, stress), integration testing with fakes/mocks, and end-to-end testing.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Use chaos engineering to inject failures, performance testing (load, stress), integration testing with fakes/mocks, and end-to-end testing.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
