    slug: "idempotency-design",
    shortDescription: "Explain idempotent operations.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIdempotency ensures that performing an operation multiple times has the same effect as doing it once. Implement using idempotency keys (e.g., client-provided UUID) stored to prevent duplicate processing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the actual procedure behind how do you design a system for idempotency, including prerequisites, the important state changes, and how you would verify the result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Idempotency ensures that performing an operation multiple times has the same effect as doing it once.\n2. Implement using idempotency keys (e.g., client-provided UUID) stored to prevent duplicate processing.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nGive the normal path first, then mention the point where the operation can fail or become unsafe.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, an engineer in a production application can follow the operation in order, verify each state change, and compare the final result with the expected behavior. Idempotency ensures that performing an operation multiple times has the same effect as doing it once.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Idempotency ensures that performing an operation multiple times has the same effect as doing it once.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Giving commands or steps without explaining what state they change.\n- Skipping a prerequisite or verification step.\n- Ignoring the main failure mode or destructive side effect.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the procedure and name the verification step. That shows you understand the operation rather than memorizing a command sequence.",
