    slug: "cache-aside-vs-read-through",
    shortDescription: "Compare the two.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCache-aside: the application manages the cache (checks cache, if miss, queries DB and populates cache). Read-through: the cache itself is responsible for loading data from the DB when a miss occurs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a cache-aside and a read-through pattern and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Cache-aside: the application manages the cache (checks cache, if miss, queries DB and populates cache).\n2. Read-through: the cache itself is responsible for loading data from the DB when a miss occurs.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Cache-aside: the application manages the cache (checks cache, if miss, queries DB and populates cache).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Cache-aside: the application manages the cache (checks cache, if miss, queries DB and populates cache).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
