    shortDescription: "Compare the two main engines.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nInnoDB supports transactions, ACID, row-level locking, foreign keys, and crash recovery. MyISAM does not support transactions or foreign keys, uses table-level locking, and is faster for read-only or low-write workloads. InnoDB is recommended for most applications.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between innodb and myisam and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. InnoDB supports transactions, ACID, row-level locking, foreign keys, and crash recovery.\n2. MyISAM does not support transactions or foreign keys, uses table-level locking, and is faster for read-only or low-write workloads.\n3. InnoDB is recommended for most applications.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. InnoDB supports transactions, ACID, row-level locking, foreign keys, and crash recovery.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. InnoDB supports transactions, ACID, row-level locking, foreign keys, and crash recovery.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
