    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn index is a data structure (typically B-tree) that improves the speed of data retrieval. It is crucial for performance but adds overhead on DML operations. Indexes can be created on one or multiple columns.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain an index in MySQL? Why is it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. An index is a data structure (typically B-tree) that improves the speed of data retrieval.\n2. It is crucial for performance but adds overhead on DML operations.\n3. Indexes can be created on one or multiple columns.\n4. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor `SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ? ORDER BY created_at DESC`, an index such as `(customer_id, created_at)` may help, but the actual benefit should be confirmed from the query plan and data distribution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. An index is a data structure (typically B-tree) that improves the speed of data retrieval.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
