    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA deadlock occurs when two or more transactions hold locks and wait for each other. MySQL detects deadlocks and rolls back one of the transactions (the one that has done the least work) to break the cycle.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a deadlock and how does MySQL handle it accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A deadlock occurs when two or more transactions hold locks and wait for each other.\n2. MySQL detects deadlocks and rolls back one of the transactions (the one that has done the least work) to break the cycle.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can use the concept in a concrete query or transaction and verify the resulting rows, locks, or execution plan. A deadlock occurs when two or more transactions hold locks and wait for each other.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A deadlock occurs when two or more transactions hold locks and wait for each other.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Assuming a schema or index is automatically faster without checking access patterns.\n- Ignoring concurrency or consistency requirements.\n- Quoting performance characteristics without measured evidence.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
