    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA function returns a single value and can be used in SQL expressions. A procedure does not return a value (but can have OUT parameters) and is called with `CALL`.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a stored procedure and a function and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A function returns a single value and can be used in SQL expressions.\n2. A procedure does not return a value (but can have OUT parameters) and is called with `CALL`.\n3. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A function returns a single value and can be used in SQL expressions.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A function returns a single value and can be used in SQL expressions.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
