    slug: "view-advantages",
    shortDescription: "List benefits.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nViews provide security (hide columns), simplification (hide complex joins), data abstraction (present only necessary data), and consistency (centralize logic).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks engineering judgment around what are the advantages of using views. A useful answer makes the benefit, cost, and condition for choosing the approach explicit.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Views provide security (hide columns), simplification (hide complex joins), data abstraction (present only necessary data), and consistency (centralize logic).\n2. Tie the explanation to data shape, access pattern, concurrency, and the evidence you would use to judge performance or correctness..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nState the benefit, the cost, and the constraint that decides whether the trade-off is worth accepting.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Views provide security (hide columns), simplification (hide complex joins), data abstraction (present only necessary data), and consistency (centralize logic).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a data-backed service, what are the advantages of using views matters when correctness, access patterns, concurrency, and query or storage cost have to work together. Measure the real workload rather than assuming a design choice is always beneficial.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Listing benefits and drawbacks without saying when they matter.\n- Treating one approach as universally superior.\n- Ignoring operational cost, maintenance, or failure behavior.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nName the constraint that makes the trade-off acceptable, then state when you would deliberately choose another approach.",
