    slug: "dockerfile-expose",
    shortDescription: "Explain port exposure.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`EXPOSE` informs Docker that the container listens on the specified port at runtime. It does not publish the port; that is done with `-p` or `-P` during `docker run`. It serves as documentation and is used with `-P` for port mapping.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `EXPOSE` instruction in a Dockerfile accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `EXPOSE` informs Docker that the container listens on the specified port at runtime.\n2. It does not publish the port; that is done with `-p` or `-P` during `docker run`.\n3. It serves as documentation and is used with `-P` for port mapping.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nLead with the distinction that matters most. Then connect it to behavior, a practical consequence, and one limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `EXPOSE` informs Docker that the container listens on the specified port at runtime.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `EXPOSE` informs Docker that the container listens on the specified port at runtime.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
