    shortDescription: "Compare the two storage options.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA bind mount mounts a host directory or file into the container. A volume is a managed storage area managed by Docker. Volumes are more portable, have better performance, and are easier to backup/migrate than bind mounts.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between a docker volume and a bind mount and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A bind mount mounts a host directory or file into the container.\n2. A volume is a managed storage area managed by Docker.\n3. Volumes are more portable, have better performance, and are easier to backup/migrate than bind mounts.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nFocus on the difference that changes the engineering decision rather than comparing every feature. Use one scenario to make it concrete.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. A bind mount mounts a host directory or file into the container.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A bind mount mounts a host directory or file into the container.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
