    slug: "build-vs-commit",
    shortDescription: "Compare image creation methods.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`docker build` creates an image from a Dockerfile. `docker commit` creates an image from an existing container's changes. `docker commit` is manual and less reproducible; `docker build` is preferred.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `docker build` and `docker commit` and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `docker build` creates an image from a Dockerfile.\n2. `docker commit` creates an image from an existing container's changes.\n3. `docker commit` is manual and less reproducible; `docker build` is preferred.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `docker build` creates an image from a Dockerfile.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `docker build` creates an image from a Dockerfile.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
