    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDocker provides: `bridge` (default), `host` (shares host network), `none` (no network), `overlay` (multi-host communication), and `macvlan` (assigns MAC addresses). Additionally, third-party plugins are available.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Docker provides: `bridge` (default), `host` (shares host network), `none` (no network), `overlay` (multi-host communication), and `macvlan` (assigns MAC addresses).\n2. Additionally, third-party plugins are available.\n3. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Docker provides: `bridge` (default), `host` (shares host network), `none` (no network), `overlay` (multi-host communication), and `macvlan` (assigns MAC addresses).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a container platform, what are the different network drivers in docker affects deployment, networking, storage, health, or runtime isolation. The practical decision depends on the workload and failure/recovery requirements.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
