    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nTypes: named volumes, anonymous volumes, and bind mounts. Named volumes are managed by Docker and have a specific name. Anonymous volumes are automatically named. Bind mounts directly map host directories.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Types: named volumes, anonymous volumes, and bind mounts.\n2. Named volumes are managed by Docker and have a specific name.\n3. Anonymous volumes are automatically named.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Types: named volumes, anonymous volumes, and bind mounts.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a container platform, what are the types of docker volumes affects deployment, networking, storage, health, or runtime isolation. The practical decision depends on the workload and failure/recovery requirements.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
