    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`COPY` adds files to the image at build time. `VOLUME` declares a mount point that can be used for persistent storage at runtime. It does not copy data.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between `copy` and `volume` in a dockerfile and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `COPY` adds files to the image at build time.\n2. `VOLUME` declares a mount point that can be used for persistent storage at runtime.\n3. It does not copy data.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. `COPY` adds files to the image at build time.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `COPY` adds files to the image at build time.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
