    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n`--publish mode=ingress` publishes the service port on all nodes. Traffic is routed to the appropriate container via the Swarm routing mesh. `mode=host` publishes only on the node running the service.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the `--publish mode=ingress` in Swarm services accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. `--publish mode=ingress` publishes the service port on all nodes.\n2. Traffic is routed to the appropriate container via the Swarm routing mesh.\n3. `mode=host` publishes only on the node running the service.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. `--publish mode=ingress` publishes the service port on all nodes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. `--publish mode=ingress` publishes the service port on all nodes.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
