    slug: "docker-security",
    shortDescription: "List best practices.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRun containers as non-root (`USER`). Use minimal base images. Scan images for vulnerabilities. Use secrets management (not env vars for secrets). Use read-only root filesystems (`--read-only`). Limit container capabilities (`--cap-drop`).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks breadth, but the useful signal is whether you can explain what the important items are for and distinguish the ones that are commonly confused.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Run containers as non-root (`USER`).\n2. Use minimal base images.\n3. Scan images for vulnerabilities.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nKeep the list organized and explain one practical use or limitation for each important item.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, an engineer can apply the control to an actual request or resource boundary and verify that an unauthorized action is rejected. Run containers as non-root (`USER`).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a container platform, what are the security considerations when running docker affects deployment, networking, storage, health, or runtime isolation. The practical decision depends on the workload and failure/recovery requirements.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Reciting names without explaining their purpose.\n- Missing the common options while focusing on obscure variants.\n- Giving an exhaustive list when the question only asks for the important examples.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nGive the common answers first and attach one useful distinction or use case to each instead of reciting a long list.",
