    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nContainers run with a limited set of Linux capabilities. `--cap-drop` removes capabilities, `--cap-add` adds them. Dropping all and adding only necessary ones (e.g., `NET_ADMIN`) improves security.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain `--cap-drop` and `--cap-add` accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Containers run with a limited set of Linux capabilities.\n2. `--cap-drop` removes capabilities, `--cap-add` adds them.\n3. Dropping all and adding only necessary ones (e.g., `NET_ADMIN`) improves security.\n4. Explain what happens to the container or workload at runtime and include the relevant networking, storage, health, or rollout consequence..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Containers run with a limited set of Linux capabilities.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Containers run with a limited set of Linux capabilities.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
