    question: "What is the difference between scalability and elasticity?",
    slug: "scalability-vs-elasticity",
    shortDescription: "Define both terms and clarify their distinction.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nScalability is the ability of a system to handle increased load by adding resources (vertical or horizontal). Elasticity is the ability to automatically scale resources up and down based on demand, often in cloud environments.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between scalability and elasticity and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Scalability is the ability of a system to handle increased load by adding resources (vertical or horizontal).\n2. Elasticity is the ability to automatically scale resources up and down based on demand, often in cloud environments.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Scalability is the ability of a system to handle increased load by adding resources (vertical or horizontal).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Scalability is the ability of a system to handle increased load by adding resources (vertical or horizontal).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
