    question: "What is the difference between consistency and availability in CAP?",
    slug: "consistency-vs-availability",
    shortDescription: "Clarify the trade-offs.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nConsistency ensures all nodes see the same data at the same time. Availability ensures the system responds to requests even if some nodes are down. In a network partition, you must choose: either keep the system consistent (CP) or keep it available (AP).\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between consistency and availability in cap and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Consistency ensures all nodes see the same data at the same time.\n2. Availability ensures the system responds to requests even if some nodes are down.\n3. In a network partition, you must choose: either keep the system consistent (CP) or keep it available (AP).\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nDefine both sides briefly, state the real boundary between them, and then give the condition that would make you choose one over the other.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Consistency ensures all nodes see the same data at the same time.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Consistency ensures all nodes see the same data at the same time.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
