    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA CDN is a geographically distributed network of proxy servers that caches content (static assets) closer to end users. It reduces latency, offloads origin servers, and improves availability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a CDN and how does it improve performance accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. A CDN is a geographically distributed network of proxy servers that caches content (static assets) closer to end users.\n2. It reduces latency, offloads origin servers, and improves availability.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define it first, explain the part that actually changes the outcome, and use the example to show I understand it in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. A CDN is a geographically distributed network of proxy servers that caches content (static assets) closer to end users.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. A CDN is a geographically distributed network of proxy servers that caches content (static assets) closer to end users.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
