    question: "What is the difference between consistency models: strong, eventual, and causal?",
    slug: "consistency-models",
    shortDescription: "Define the models.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nStrong consistency: reads reflect the latest write across all nodes. Eventual consistency: after writes stop, all replicas will converge. Causal consistency: operations that are causally related are seen in the same order by all nodes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe interviewer is checking whether you can explain the boundary between the two choices in what is the difference between consistency models: strong, eventual, and causal and tie that difference to an engineering decision.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Strong consistency: reads reflect the latest write across all nodes.\n2. Eventual consistency: after writes stop, all replicas will converge.\n3. Causal consistency: operations that are causally related are seen in the same order by all nodes.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nExplain what problem each option solves, the trade-off between them, and when each is the better fit.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, choose the option whose behavior matches the requirement and compare the resulting operational consequence. Strong consistency: reads reflect the latest write across all nodes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Strong consistency: reads reflect the latest write across all nodes.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Defining both sides but never stating the practical difference.\n- Choosing one option without naming the requirement that drives the choice.\n- Treating platform-specific behavior as a universal rule.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nState the decision rule early: when would you choose A, and when would you choose B? That is more useful than listing every secondary difference.",
