    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\n3PC extends 2PC with a pre-commit phase to avoid blocking if the coordinator fails. It requires that participants can make decisions based on timeouts, making it more resilient but more complex.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain the Three-Phase Commit (3PC) protocol accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. 3PC extends 2PC with a pre-commit phase to avoid blocking if the coordinator fails.\n2. It requires that participants can make decisions based on timeouts, making it more resilient but more complex.\n3. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. 3PC extends 2PC with a pre-commit phase to avoid blocking if the coordinator fails.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. 3PC extends 2PC with a pre-commit phase to avoid blocking if the coordinator fails.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
