    question: "What is a retry with exponential backoff and why is it used?",
    slug: "exponential-backoff",
    shortDescription: "Explain the retry strategy.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nExponential backoff is a retry policy where the delay between attempts increases exponentially (e.g., 1s, 2s, 4s). It prevents thundering herd and gives the system time to recover. Used with jitter to avoid collisions.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can explain a retry with exponential backoff and why is it used accurately and connect the definition to a practical consequence. A strong answer should also acknowledge the main limitation or boundary.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Exponential backoff is a retry policy where the delay between attempts increases exponentially (e.g., 1s, 2s, 4s).\n2. It prevents thundering herd and gives the system time to recover.\n3. Used with jitter to avoid collisions.\n4. Connect the concept to latency, failure modes, capacity, consistency, and operational cost rather than discussing components in isolation..\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the answer in one sentence, then explain the mechanism that makes it true. Give one concrete example and stop before drifting into unrelated details.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a production application, a team can apply this concept to a concrete requirement and verify the behavior described in the answer. Exponential backoff is a retry policy where the delay between attempts increases exponentially (e.g., 1s, 2s, 4s).\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nIn a production application, engineers use this when they need the behavior described by the question. Exponential backoff is a retry policy where the delay between attempts increases exponentially (e.g., 1s, 2s, 4s).\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Stopping at the definition without explaining how the concept behaves.\n- Using an example that does not actually demonstrate the point.\n- Making an absolute claim when configuration, version, workload, or context changes the result.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nStay close to the exact question. One technically precise example is more valuable than a broad overview of the entire technology.",
